= Introduction to competitive programming
== Target audience and the goal of this project
This project aims to introduce various concepts and aspects of competitive programming to incoming freshmen who have background in programming.

Therefore, this project will assume that the audience is comfortable with the syntax of at least 1 programming language of their choice (preferably C++ or Python, which are the two main languages used in the field of competitive programming). 

Also, for the math portion of the content, this project will assume that the audience will have knowledge of basic algebra (pre-Calc in terms of math curriculum), and some knowledge (or ability to research on their own) of symbolic logic and proof strategies. In UCI, such discrete math knolwedge is is covered primarily on 6B and 6D at the point I am writing this document - note that knowledge from these courses are not a strict pre-requisite, since I will aim to provide sources that you can learn such concepts and with some explanation if needed.

Note that content will be ordered in a sequential manner in which I think would be suitable for people who are starting to learn competitive programming - therefore later content will assume knowledge of prior contents, although being in order is not a strict requirement (You can read sections about dynamic programming in advance if you are particularly intersted in it.).